{#
	The content part of an invitation (XIV-1), drawn inside the same frame every
	other email from this application uses — `@XiviCore/email/base.html.twig`,
	which is what supplies the skeleton, the width and the footer naming the
	company sending it (§5.13). This file is only what goes in the middle, and it
	is rendered to a string and handed in as `content` by `UserInvitations`.

	Inline styles for the same reason the frame uses them: Gmail and Outlook both
	drop a <style> block often enough that relying on one is relying on luck. Not
	the house Bootstrap style, and not meant to be.

	**The URL appears twice on purpose** — once as the button and once as text
	below it. A client that refuses to render the anchor, or a reader forwarding
	this to the machine they actually work on, still has something to copy. It is
	the one thing this message exists to deliver.
#}
<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'invitation.greeting'|trans({'%name%': name}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 24px 0;">{{ 'invitation.body'|trans({'%instance%': instance}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 24px 0;">
	<a href="{{ url }}" style="display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background-color: #0d6efd; color: #ffffff; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: 600;">
		{{ 'invitation.button'|trans }}
	</a>
</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 24px 0; word-break: break-all; font-size: 13px; color: #6c757d;">
	{{ url }}
</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">{{ 'invitation.expiry'|trans({'%hours%': hours}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'invitation.once'|trans }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0; color: #6c757d; font-size: 13px;">{{ 'invitation.unexpected'|trans }}</p>
